**Day 21: Introduction to NoSQL and MongoDB**  

**Topic:**  
Introduction to NoSQL databases, focusing on MongoDB as a document-oriented database. Explanation of schema flexibility, scalability, and use cases where NoSQL outperforms traditional relational databases.  

**Learning Objectives:**  
• Understand the fundamentals of NoSQL databases and their advantages.  
• Learn the basic structure and operations of MongoDB.  
• Compare NoSQL and SQL databases for different scenarios.  

**Activities / Deliverables:**  
• Install MongoDB and set up a local instance.  
• Perform basic CRUD operations using MongoDB Shell.  
• Create a simple document-based database for a sample application.  

**Important Concepts:**  
• Document-oriented storage (BSON format).  
• Horizontal scalability and sharding.  
• Dynamic schema vs. rigid schema in SQL.  

**SQL Syntax / Commands:**  
• `db.collection.insertOne({document})` – Inserts a single document.  
• `db.collection.find({query})` – Retrieves documents matching criteria.  
• `db.collection.updateOne({filter}, {update})` – Updates a document.  

**Real-world Use Cases:**  
• Handling large-scale, unstructured data (e.g., social media posts).  
• Real-time analytics and high-velocity data logging.  

**Tools / Tech:**  
• MongoDB Community Server  
• MongoDB Compass (GUI)  
• MongoDB Shell  

**Summary:**  
Explored NoSQL fundamentals and MongoDB’s document-based approach, practiced basic operations, and compared use cases with SQL databases.  

